[Event "Chapter 1: White Plays 5.Nf3 / Study 1: White Plays 8.0-0 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/840e407d-54cc-45e3-9f3e-bff84a26d66d/chapters/305de3e9-b77f-4008-a0a8-31f7c676993d/studies/c59f5a62-14bf-4d5a-87b5-1450e75158fc/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of Chapter 1 in the Petroff Gotham Gambit. In this study, we’ll explore lines where White plays 8.0-0, and we respond with an aggressive approach. White opens with the classic 1.e4, grabbing space and opening lines for development. We reply symmetrically with 1...e5, staking our own claim to the center.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3, immediately attacking our e5-pawn. We enter the Petroff Defense with 2...Nf6 — a direct counter to e4. Instead of defending, we counterattack. This defense appears in around 12% of games.} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White takes on e5 more than 30% of the time. But we’re ready to launch... The Petroff Gotham Gambit! With 3...d5, which is played in just 1% of games, we challenge White immediately and steer them away from main line theory. If White accepts, they remain a pawn up... for now.} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White captures again with 4.exd5, opening the center. Around 65% of games include this move. We’re now two pawns down, but we’ll recover one immediately and aim to compensate the remaining pawn with piece activity and initiative. We recapture with the queen, centralizing it and hitting the knight.} 4...Qxd5 5.Nf3 {The knight retreats to f3, getting out of danger. This is the most popular response by White, played nearly 40% of the time. We continue developing with 5...Nc6. Yes, we’re still a pawn down, but we already have three pieces out and easy development ahead. Our plan is simple: play ...Bg4 and long castle.} 5...Nc6 6.Nc3 {White plays 6.Nc3, developing with tempo. This move is played 56% of the time. If we respond correctly, Black wins nearly 60% of games from this position. We play 6...Qh5! The queen stays active, adds pressure on f3, and supports future ideas like ...Bg4 and ...O-O-O.} 6...Qh5 7.Be2 {White develops with 7.Be2, adding a defender to the knight and preparing to castle. It’s the most natural response in this position. We stick to our plan, developing the light-squared bishop to its ideal square.} 7...Bg4 8.O-O {White castles with 8.0-0. That's the most popular move, but not the best. According to the engine, Black has already equalized. And we continue with our development. At this point, all our minor pieces and the queen are developed. We’re fully mobilized and ready to castle long.} 8...Bd6 9.h3 {White plays 9.h3, asking our bishop to decide: trade or retreat? We choose neither and castle long! This brings the king to safety, connects the rooks, and sets up possible threats against h2. Alternatively, 9...Bxh3 is a less ambitious option — sacrificing the bishop with the idea of forcing a draw by threefold repetition.} 9...O-O-O 10.d4 {This is White’s best option. They take space in the center, shut down any ...Nd4 ideas, and open up their dark-squared bishop. But we sacrifice the bishop anyway! It’s not the best move, but a very interesting one. We want to open up the White king and induce a mistake.} 10...Bxh3 11.Ne5 {This is White’s best move, but it’s a hard one to find. It’s a discovered attack on our queen and a strong defensive resource.} 11...Qh4 {We shift the queen forward to stay active and keep up the pressure. Capturing the bishop would be a blunder. The best move for White now is 12.g3, which would force us to take on d4. The position is +1.2 for White, but we equalize material, and the sequence of moves up to this point is not easy to find.} *

