1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of Chapter 1 in the Petroff Gotham Gambit. In this study, we’ll explore lines where White plays 8.0-0, and we respond with an aggressive approach. White opens with the classic 1.e4, grabbing space and opening lines for development. We reply symmetrically with 1...e5, staking our own claim to the center.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3, immediately attacking our e5-pawn. We enter the Petroff Defense with 2...Nf6 — a direct counter to e4. Instead of defending, we counterattack. This defense appears in around 12% of games.} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White takes on e5 more than 30% of the time. But we’re ready to launch... The Petroff Gotham Gambit! With 3...d5, which is played in just 1% of games, we challenge White immediately and steer them away from main line theory. If White accepts, they remain a pawn up... for now.} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White captures again with 4.exd5, opening the center. Around 65% of games include this move. We’re now two pawns down, but we’ll recover one immediately and aim to compensate the remaining pawn with piece activity and initiative. We recapture with the queen, centralizing it and hitting the knight.} 4...Qxd5 5.Nf3 {The knight retreats to f3, getting out of danger. This is the most popular response by White, played nearly 40% of the time. We continue developing with 5...Nc6. Yes, we’re still a pawn down, but we already have three pieces out and easy development ahead. Our plan is simple: play ...Bg4 and long castle.} 5...Nc6 6.Nc3 {White plays 6.Nc3, developing with tempo. This move is played 56% of the time. If we respond correctly, Black wins nearly 60% of games from this position. We play 6...Qh5! The queen stays active, adds pressure on f3, and supports future ideas like ...Bg4 and ...O-O-O.} 6...Qh5 7.Be2 {White develops with 7.Be2, adding a defender to the knight and preparing to castle. It’s the most natural response in this position. We stick to our plan, developing the light-squared bishop to its ideal square.} 7...Bg4 8.O-O {White castles with 8.0-0. That's the most popular move, but not the best. According to the engine, Black has already equalized. And we continue with our development. At this point, all our minor pieces and the queen are developed. We’re fully mobilized and ready to castle long.} 8...Bd6 9.h3 {White plays 9.h3, asking our bishop to decide: trade or retreat? We choose neither and castle long! This brings the king to safety, connects the rooks, and sets up possible threats against h2. Alternatively, 9...Bxh3 is a less ambitious option — sacrificing the bishop with the idea of forcing a draw by threefold repetition.} 9...O-O-O 10.Nh2 {White might be aiming to simplify by inviting trades, but it’s not the best option. It leaves us with a slightly better position. We go ahead and trade the light-squared bishops.} 10...Bxe2 11.Nxe2 {White could also recapture with the knight instead.} 11...Rhe8 {In that case, we keep adding pressure. We’re still a pawn down, but we are miles ahead in development. 12.Nc3 Nb4 is an idea to win c2. This position is definitely not easy for White. 12.Nf3 g5 with the idea of ...g4 is also a great variation for us.} *

