1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of Chapter 1 in the Petroff Gotham Gambit. In this study, we’ll explore lines where White plays 8.0-0, and we respond with an aggressive approach. White opens with the classic 1.e4, grabbing space and opening lines for development. We reply symmetrically with 1...e5, staking our own claim to the center.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3, immediately attacking our e5-pawn. We enter the Petroff Defense with 2...Nf6 — a direct counter to e4. Instead of defending, we counterattack. This defense appears in around 12% of games.} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White takes on e5 more than 30% of the time. But we’re ready to launch... The Petroff Gotham Gambit! With 3...d5, which is played in just 1% of games, we challenge White immediately and steer them away from main line theory. If White accepts, they remain a pawn up... for now.} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White captures again with 4.exd5, opening the center. Around 65% of games include this move. We’re now two pawns down, but we’ll recover one immediately and aim to compensate the remaining pawn with piece activity and initiative. We recapture with the queen, centralizing it and hitting the knight.} 4...Qxd5 5.Nf3 {The knight retreats to f3, getting out of danger. This is the most popular response by White, played nearly 40% of the time. We continue developing with 5...Nc6. Yes, we’re still a pawn down, but we already have three pieces out and easy development ahead. Our plan is simple: play ...Bg4 and long castle.} 5...Nc6 6.Nc3 {White plays 6.Nc3, developing with tempo. This move is played 56% of the time. If we respond correctly, Black wins nearly 60% of games from this position. We play 6...Qh5! The queen stays active, adds pressure on f3, and supports future ideas like ...Bg4 and ...O-O-O.} 6...Qh5 7.Be2 {White develops with 7.Be2, adding a defender to the knight and preparing to castle. It’s the most natural response in this position. We stick to our plan, developing the light-squared bishop to its ideal square.} 7...Bg4 8.O-O {White castles with 8.0-0. That's the most popular move, but not the best. According to the engine, Black has already equalized. And we continue with our development. At this point, all our minor pieces and the queen are developed. We’re fully mobilized and ready to castle long.} 8...Bd6 9.g3 {White blocks the dark-squared bishop’s diagonal, but weakens the light squares around the king. Black now has the initiative and, according to the engine, a clear edge — roughly -1.0. We stay true to our plan: castling long, bringing the king to safety, and connecting the rooks.} 9...O-O-O 10.d3 {White opens up their dark-squared bishop, which is now ready to be developed. We bring the other rook into the game. Now all of our pieces are developed and active. We’re threatening ...Rxe2, which could win two pieces for a rook while also taking control of the light squares near White’s king.} 10...Rhe8 11.Be3 {White acknowledges the threat and develops the bishop to block the rook’s influence on the e-file. The best move in the position! This pawn push invites White to capture, potentially giving up two pieces for a rook and a pawn, and even opens up possibilities for a piece sacrifice to rip open the king’s defenses.} 11...g5 12.Nxg5 {White accepts the pawn, but this allows us to simplify, and we still remain better. Taking with the bishop would’ve been worse: after ...Rxe2 and ...Bxf3, Black would have enormous pressure and a -4.5 evaluation. We start by capturing the dark-squared bishop, which is the only defender of the g5-knight.} 12...Rxe3 13.fxe3 {White takes the rook.} 13...Qxg5 {And we take the knight. We’re still down a point of material, but our pieces are more active, and our king is significantly safer. If 14.Rxf6, we should first capture the bishop with tempo on the queen and then take the rook. If 14.Bxg4, we simply recapture with the knight.} *

