[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/840e407d-54cc-45e3-9f3e-bff84a26d66d/chapters/305de3e9-b77f-4008-a0a8-31f7c676993d/studies/c59f5a62-14bf-4d5a-87b5-1450e75158fc/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the first study of Chapter 1 in the Petroff Gotham Gambit. In this study, we’ll explore lines where White plays 8.0-0, and we respond with an aggressive approach. White opens with the classic 1.e4, grabbing space and opening lines for development. We reply symmetrically with 1...e5, staking our own claim to the center.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3, immediately attacking our e5-pawn. We enter the Petroff Defense with 2...Nf6 — a direct counter to e4. Instead of defending, we counterattack. This defense appears in around 12% of games.} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White takes on e5 more than 30% of the time. But we’re ready to launch... The Petroff Gotham Gambit! With 3...d5, which is played in just 1% of games, we challenge White immediately and steer them away from main line theory. If White accepts, they remain a pawn up... for now.} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White captures again with 4.exd5, opening the center. Around 65% of games include this move. We’re now two pawns down, but we’ll recover one immediately and aim to compensate the remaining pawn with piece activity and initiative. We recapture with the queen, centralizing it and hitting the knight.} 4...Qxd5 5.Nf3 {The knight retreats to f3, getting out of danger. This is the most popular response by White, played nearly 40% of the time. We continue developing with 5...Nc6. Yes, we’re still a pawn down, but we already have three pieces out and easy development ahead. Our plan is simple: play ...Bg4 and long castle.} 5...Nc6 6.Nc3 {White plays 6.Nc3, developing with tempo. This move is played 56% of the time. If we respond correctly, Black wins nearly 60% of games from this position. We play 6...Qh5! The queen stays active, adds pressure on f3, and supports future ideas like ...Bg4 and ...O-O-O.} 6...Qh5 7.Be2 {White develops with 7.Be2, adding a defender to the knight and preparing to castle. It’s the most natural response in this position. We stick to our plan, developing the light-squared bishop to its ideal square.} 7...Bg4 8.O-O {White castles with 8.0-0. That's the most popular move, but not the best. According to the engine, Black has already equalized. And we continue with our development. At this point, all our minor pieces and the queen are developed. We’re fully mobilized and ready to castle long.} 8...Bd6 9.g3 {White blocks the dark-squared bishop’s diagonal, but weakens the light squares around the king. Black now has the initiative and, according to the engine, a clear edge — roughly -1.0. We stay true to our plan: castling long, bringing the king to safety, and connecting the rooks.} 9...O-O-O 10.d3 {White opens up their dark-squared bishop, which is now ready to be developed. We bring the other rook into the game. Now all of our pieces are developed and active. We’re threatening ...Rxe2, which could win two pieces for a rook while also taking control of the light squares near White’s king.} 10...Rhe8 11.Be3 {White acknowledges the threat and develops the bishop to block the rook’s influence on the e-file. The best move in the position! This pawn push invites White to capture, potentially giving up two pieces for a rook and a pawn, and even opens up possibilities for a piece sacrifice to rip open the king’s defenses.} 11...g5 12.Re1 {White declines the pawn and develops the rook to e1, adding a defender to the bishop on e2. But… A brilliant bishop sacrifice! The goal: rip open the center and expose White’s king.} 12...Bf4 13.gxf4 {White’s best response is to accept the sacrifice. We capture the pawn, deflecting the dark-squared bishop from the e-file and reactivating our rook’s threat against e2.} 13...gxf4 14.Bxf4 {White regains the pawn, but the light-squared bishop is now under pressure.} 14...Rxe2 {We unleash an exchange sacrifice! If White recaptures with the rook, we follow up with ...Bxf3. Even though we’re down the exchange and a pawn, Black stands better. White’s king is wide open and difficult to defend. Black has an advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White Plays 5.Nf3 / Study 2: White Plays 8.h3 or d3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/840e407d-54cc-45e3-9f3e-bff84a26d66d/chapters/305de3e9-b77f-4008-a0a8-31f7c676993d/studies/23382120-0fb6-4110-8f24-53f03d929fff/lines"]

1.e4 {For this second study, we will continue our exploration of the accepted gambit with the retreat 5.Nf3. We will explore setups with 8.d3 or 8.h3. White begins with the most common first move, claiming space in the center. We mirror White’s move.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops the knight and puts pressure on our e5-pawn. We enter the Petroff Defense, a symmetrical response that counterattacks e4 rather than defending e5 directly.} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White grabs the pawn on e5. This is the Petroff Gotham Gambit!} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White accepts the pawn. We recapture with the queen, centralizing it and placing immediate pressure on the knight.} 4...Qxd5 5.Nf3 {The knight returns to f3, stepping out of danger. We continue to bring our pieces out. Even though we're still down a pawn, our rapid development puts us ahead on activity.} 5...Nc6 6.Nc3 {White keeps developing logically, and now the queen must move again, as it's under attack. We slide the queen over to h5, keeping it active and pressuring the f3-knight. This also supports future ideas like ...Bg4 and castling long.} 6...Qh5 7.Be2 {White plays 7.Be2, reinforcing the knight on f3 and getting ready to castle. We stick to the plan and activate the light-squared bishop.} 7...Bg4 8.h3 {White asks our bishop a question, but is it really a threat? Not quite, since if they take the bishop, we simply capture the hanging rook on h1. We stick to our plan and castle long. Development is nearly complete, and we’re ready to bring every piece into play.} 8...O-O-O 9.d3 {White opens the diagonal for their dark-squared bishop. We develop our bishop to its ideal square.} 9...Bd6 10.Be3 {White brings out their dark-squared bishop as well. We bring our h-rook to the center, occupying the open file. All of our pieces are now developed and active.} 10...Rhe8 11.Qd2 {White connects rooks and prepares to castle long. We pin the knight to the queen, improving the position of our dark-squared bishop and adding pressure to the c3-knight. The position is now equal.} 11...Bb4 12.O-O-O {White castles long, taking a different approach to all the other lines we’ve seen in the chapter, but this turns out to be a mistake! With precise play, Black can seize the advantage here. A brilliant queen maneuver! We’ve just hung the bishop, but it’s all part of the plan. The queen swings to the queenside, increasing the pressure on c3 and preparing either ...Ne4 (thanks to the pinned d-pawn) or ...Nd5.} 12...Qa5 13.hxg4 {White accepts the bishop and is now up four points of material. But things aren’t so simple. We continue the assault by increasing pressure on c3. White is materially ahead, but their position is crumbling. Note: 13...Ne4 doesn't work — White can simply take the knight, and after 14...Rxd2 15.Bxd2, they’ll be winning with two pieces and a rook for the queen, plus a pawn advantage.} 13...Nd5 14.Rh5 {White tries a creative defensive resource, pinning the d5-knight to our queen. But this allows us to simplify and emerge up an exchange. We capture the knight on c3 first, hitting the queen.} 14...Bxc3 15.Qxc3 {White recaptures with the queen. Capturing with the b-pawn would have been fatal due to ...Qxa2, and the king would be completely exposed. We take the queen with the knight, which is the only winning continuation for Black.} 15...Nxc3 16.Rxa5 {White takes our queen. They’re still up four points of material. However, both of their rooks are available for capture, and choosing the right one can shift the balance in our favor.} 16...Nxd1 {We choose the d1-rook. If White captures our knight, we simply take the other rook and emerge two exchanges ahead. If they try to save their rook, we go for ...Nxf2 followed by ...Rxe2 and come out a full exchange up.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White Plays 5.Nf3 / Study 2: White Plays 8.h3 or d3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/840e407d-54cc-45e3-9f3e-bff84a26d66d/chapters/305de3e9-b77f-4008-a0a8-31f7c676993d/studies/23382120-0fb6-4110-8f24-53f03d929fff/lines"]

1.e4 {For this second study, we will continue our exploration of the accepted gambit with the retreat 5.Nf3. We will explore setups with 8.d3 or 8.h3. White begins with the most common first move, claiming space in the center. We mirror White’s move.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops the knight and puts pressure on our e5-pawn. We enter the Petroff Defense, a symmetrical response that counterattacks e4 rather than defending e5 directly.} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White grabs the pawn on e5. This is the Petroff Gotham Gambit!} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White accepts the pawn. We recapture with the queen, centralizing it and placing immediate pressure on the knight.} 4...Qxd5 5.Nf3 {The knight returns to f3, stepping out of danger. We continue to bring our pieces out. Even though we're still down a pawn, our rapid development puts us ahead on activity.} 5...Nc6 6.Nc3 {White keeps developing logically, and now the queen must move again, as it's under attack. We slide the queen over to h5, keeping it active and pressuring the f3-knight. This also supports future ideas like ...Bg4 and castling long.} 6...Qh5 7.Be2 {White plays 7.Be2, reinforcing the knight on f3 and getting ready to castle. We stick to the plan and activate the light-squared bishop.} 7...Bg4 8.d3 {White’s other option is to open up the diagonal for their dark-squared bishop. We follow our usual plan, castling long.} 8...O-O-O 9.Be3 {White continues developing, bringing out the dark-squared bishop. We also develop our bishop, this time to b4, pinning the knight to the king.} 9...Bb4 10.a3 {White challenges our bishop. Instead of retreating, we take the knight and damage White’s queenside pawn structure.} 10...Bxc3+ 11.bxc3 {White recaptures, accepting the doubled pawns in return for the bishop pair.} 11...Nd5 {We improve the knight by jumping to d5, targeting the weak c3-pawn. Next, we’ll likely bring the h-rook to the center, and can even look for a rook lift with ...Rd6.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White Plays 5.Nf3 / Study 2: White Plays 8.h3 or d3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/840e407d-54cc-45e3-9f3e-bff84a26d66d/chapters/305de3e9-b77f-4008-a0a8-31f7c676993d/studies/23382120-0fb6-4110-8f24-53f03d929fff/lines"]

1.e4 {For this second study, we will continue our exploration of the accepted gambit with the retreat 5.Nf3. We will explore setups with 8.d3 or 8.h3. White begins with the most common first move, claiming space in the center. We mirror White’s move.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops the knight and puts pressure on our e5-pawn. We enter the Petroff Defense, a symmetrical response that counterattacks e4 rather than defending e5 directly.} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White grabs the pawn on e5. This is the Petroff Gotham Gambit!} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White accepts the pawn. We recapture with the queen, centralizing it and placing immediate pressure on the knight.} 4...Qxd5 5.Nf3 {The knight returns to f3, stepping out of danger. We continue to bring our pieces out. Even though we're still down a pawn, our rapid development puts us ahead on activity.} 5...Nc6 6.Nc3 {White keeps developing logically, and now the queen must move again, as it's under attack. We slide the queen over to h5, keeping it active and pressuring the f3-knight. This also supports future ideas like ...Bg4 and castling long.} 6...Qh5 7.Bb5 {White chooses not to defend the f3-knight and instead develops the bishop to a more active square. No problem for us as we still develop the bishop to g4. We’re not worried about the potential doubled pawns.} 7...Bg4 8.Bxc6+ {White exchanges bishop for knight, aiming to damage our pawn structure. We recapture the piece and accept the structural damage.} 8...bxc6 9.d3 {White opens the diagonal for the dark-squared bishop.} 9...Bd6 {We develop our bishop and complete minor piece development. Despite being down material, the position is equal. All our minor pieces and the queen are active, we’ll likely castle short soon, and we have two open files to play with.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White Plays 5.Nf3 / Study 2: White Plays 8.h3 or d3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/840e407d-54cc-45e3-9f3e-bff84a26d66d/chapters/305de3e9-b77f-4008-a0a8-31f7c676993d/studies/23382120-0fb6-4110-8f24-53f03d929fff/lines"]

1.e4 {For this second study, we will continue our exploration of the accepted gambit with the retreat 5.Nf3. We will explore setups with 8.d3 or 8.h3. White begins with the most common first move, claiming space in the center. We mirror White’s move.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops the knight and puts pressure on our e5-pawn. We enter the Petroff Defense, a symmetrical response that counterattacks e4 rather than defending e5 directly.} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White grabs the pawn on e5. This is the Petroff Gotham Gambit!} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White accepts the pawn. We recapture with the queen, centralizing it and placing immediate pressure on the knight.} 4...Qxd5 5.Nf3 {The knight returns to f3, stepping out of danger. We continue to bring our pieces out. Even though we're still down a pawn, our rapid development puts us ahead on activity.} 5...Nc6 6.Be2 {Instead of gaining a tempo on the queen, White opts to quickly develop the light-squared bishop to castle short as soon as possible. This is a small sideline, with 15% of games going like this. We stick to our plan and develop the light-squared bishop to g4, increasing pressure on the f3-knight.} 6...Bg4 7.O-O {White completes their plan and castles kingside, bringing the king to safety.} 7...O-O-O {We follow suit by castling long. With opposite-side castling now on the board, we have more active pieces and better development, even if we’re still a pawn down. Our next move will likely be 8...Bd6. If 8.h3, we’ll enter familiar territory from the previous study, and respond with ...Qh5.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Plays 5.d4 / Study 1: White Plays 6.Nxc6 and 7.Nc3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/840e407d-54cc-45e3-9f3e-bff84a26d66d/chapters/992babe1-f1e4-4783-8154-0d1436b9c194/studies/9d88a79a-ebd8-4d7d-965d-730886798aa3/lines"]

1.e4 {This chapter is all about White defending their knight with 5.d4. In this study, we will look at how to maximize our initiative after 6.Nxc6 and 7.Nc3, and also to respond after the less common 6.Bxc4 and 6.Nc3. White begins with 1.e4. So we claim the dark squares in similar fashion.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {Challenging the dark squares. Instead of defending, we counterattack!} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White takes the material and challenges us to prove our point. Normally, we would kick out White's invading knight and then regain our pawn. But, instead we offer White a pawn in exchange for development speed and the initiative!} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White accepts the gambit, hoping for an easy advantage. Allowing White a second pawn on d5 would be too generous, so we take it with the most active piece, forcing White to do something about the knight.} 4...Qxd5 5.d4 {White plays d4, defending the knight and controlling the center. It's one of the top three choices here at 32%, so we will be seeing it quite often. We bring the knight out to challenge and pressure the center, and White has to consider that we threaten to win the pawn back. Black is winning more than half of their games after ...Nc6.} 5...Nc6 6.Nxc6 {This is the most popular way to respond, and it happens 45% of the time. The point is to trade Black's attacker. We don't want to ruin the pawn structure unnecessarily, and we step out of tempo moves like Nc3 and c4, so we take with the queen. Also, notice the pressure on g2, making it harder for the bishop on f1 to move.} 6...Qxc6 7.Nc3 {By far the most popular with 56% of the games following this line, White develops the knight with a threat. Let's go for a clever trick. Instead of making a slow move like ...a6, we use tactics to both defend the threat and prepare to castle. 8.Bb5 will not work.} 7...Bb4 8.Qe2+ {Less than 10% of games go like this. White tries the same tempo trick on us, intending to gain time on our king and quickly castle queenside. It looks like we will have to play ...Be6 and accept White's plan... But ...Ne4 works in our favor! The tactics on c3 again prevent White from exploiting the temporary pin. 9.f3 Bxc3+ wins for Black.} 8...Ne4 9.Bd2 {White unpins, again threatening to win our pinned knight. So we remove an attacker.} 9...Bxc3 10.Bxc3 {And White recaptures. We needed to unpin to avoid losing the knight, and castling brings the rook out and tucks the king in a safer spot. Black's weaknesses are resolved, and White still has problems to fix.} 10...O-O 11.Qc4 {White tries to solve both issues and stay up a pawn by trading queens, but this is a mistake! Qf3 was the only move for equality.} 11...Qf6 {And since we aren't on White's team, why should we cooperate? White's king is stranded in the center and must make some material concession to escape, so we attack the weak f2-pawn. Black is both practically and objectively better despite being a pawn down. The evaluation is -1.6. 12.f3 Qh4+ and White must move the king or lose material.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Plays 5.d4 / Study 1: White Plays 6.Nxc6 and 7.Nc3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/840e407d-54cc-45e3-9f3e-bff84a26d66d/chapters/992babe1-f1e4-4783-8154-0d1436b9c194/studies/9d88a79a-ebd8-4d7d-965d-730886798aa3/lines"]

1.e4 {This chapter is all about White defending their knight with 5.d4. In this study, we will look at how to maximize our initiative after 6.Nxc6 and 7.Nc3, and also to respond after the less common 6.Bxc4 and 6.Nc3. White begins with 1.e4. So we claim the dark squares in similar fashion.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {Challenging the dark squares. Instead of defending, we counterattack!} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White takes the material and challenges us to prove our point. Normally, we would kick out White's invading knight and then regain our pawn. But, instead we offer White a pawn in exchange for development speed and the initiative!} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White accepts the gambit, hoping for an easy advantage. Allowing White a second pawn on d5 would be too generous, so we take it with the most active piece, forcing White to do something about the knight.} 4...Qxd5 5.d4 {White plays d4, defending the knight and controlling the center. It's one of the top three choices here at 32%, so we will be seeing it quite often. We bring the knight out to challenge and pressure the center, and White has to consider that we threaten to win the pawn back. Black is winning more than half of their games after ...Nc6.} 5...Nc6 6.Nxc6 {This is the most popular way to respond, and it happens 45% of the time. The point is to trade Black's attacker. We don't want to ruin the pawn structure unnecessarily, and we step out of tempo moves like Nc3 and c4, so we take with the queen. Also, notice the pressure on g2, making it harder for the bishop on f1 to move.} 6...Qxc6 7.Nc3 {By far the most popular with 56% of the games following this line, White develops the knight with a threat. Let's go for a clever trick. Instead of making a slow move like ...a6, we use tactics to both defend the threat and prepare to castle. 8.Bb5 will not work.} 7...Bb4 8.Bd2 {Black was threatening to take on c3 twice, so White goes for Bd2. You'll see Bd2 about 78% of the time, and Bb5 10%. But both of those moves are mistakes! Castling deals with the weak king and brings another attacker. White's king is looking very vulnerable now. For example: 9.a3 Bxc3 and we will continue with ...Re8+. White loses.} 8...O-O 9.Qf3 {20% of games continue like this, where White tries to hold onto the material and castle, but that will only make a bad situation worse. Of course, we throw in the check on e8! Not only do we gain time, but we also forcibly lure a piece where alignment with two other targets converges.} 9...Re8+ 10.Be2 {White has nothing better than Be2 to block the check. Playing Be3 would have allowed ...Bxc3+. Ne2 might avoid losing pieces, but after Black plays ...Qxc2, White goes down a pawn with the kingside pieces out of play and a lost position. Removing a defender of e2. 10...Bxc3 is a tempo move that forces white to recapture on c3 or lose a piece.} 10...Bxc3 11.Bxc3 {If White ever moves the queen away from f3, we can capture the pawn on g2. So they recapture with the bishop. And the stars align! White can't avoid losing material now.} 11...Bg4 12.Qxc6 {White can still test us, so we mustn't fall asleep. Playing ...bxc6 here would blunder everything away! White would simply have f3.} 12...Rxe2+ {Black is better, but can't hold onto the extra piece because of f3 or h3. Instead, Black will have good chances in the endgame being up a pawn with a good knight vs a bad bishop. The evaluation is -2.2.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Plays 5.d4 / Study 1: White Plays 6.Nxc6 and 7.Nc3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/840e407d-54cc-45e3-9f3e-bff84a26d66d/chapters/992babe1-f1e4-4783-8154-0d1436b9c194/studies/9d88a79a-ebd8-4d7d-965d-730886798aa3/lines"]

1.e4 {This chapter is all about White defending their knight with 5.d4. In this study, we will look at how to maximize our initiative after 6.Nxc6 and 7.Nc3, and also to respond after the less common 6.Bxc4 and 6.Nc3. White begins with 1.e4. So we claim the dark squares in similar fashion.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {Challenging the dark squares. Instead of defending, we counterattack!} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White takes the material and challenges us to prove our point. Normally, we would kick out White's invading knight and then regain our pawn. But, instead we offer White a pawn in exchange for development speed and the initiative!} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White accepts the gambit, hoping for an easy advantage. Allowing White a second pawn on d5 would be too generous, so we take it with the most active piece, forcing White to do something about the knight.} 4...Qxd5 5.d4 {White plays d4, defending the knight and controlling the center. It's one of the top three choices here at 32%, so we will be seeing it quite often. We bring the knight out to challenge and pressure the center, and White has to consider that we threaten to win the pawn back. Black is winning more than half of their games after ...Nc6.} 5...Nc6 6.Nxc6 {This is the most popular way to respond, and it happens 45% of the time. The point is to trade Black's attacker. We don't want to ruin the pawn structure unnecessarily, and we step out of tempo moves like Nc3 and c4, so we take with the queen. Also, notice the pressure on g2, making it harder for the bishop on f1 to move.} 6...Qxc6 7.Nc3 {By far the most popular with 56% of the games following this line, White develops the knight with a threat. Let's go for a clever trick. Instead of making a slow move like ...a6, we use tactics to both defend the threat and prepare to castle. 8.Bb5 will not work.} 7...Bb4 8.Bd2 {Black was threatening to take on c3 twice, so White goes for Bd2. You'll see Bd2 about 78% of the time, and Bb5 10%. But both of those moves are mistakes! Castling deals with the weak king and brings another attacker. White's king is looking very vulnerable now. For example: 9.a3 Bxc3 and we will continue with ...Re8+. White loses.} 8...O-O 9.Be2 {Be2, giving away the g2-pawn, is the most common attempt at survival, with 38% of players choosing the move. However, it is losing. Black finally regains material equality. White's activity is illusory.} 9...Qxg2 10.Bf3 {Defending the rook but opening up the e-file. The queen might seem a bit oddly placed, but it is actively putting pressure on White, and the g4-square is important for later.} 10...Qh3 11.Qe2 {White prepares to castle queenside. Be3 first is a bit more popular, but mostly just transposes to the same line. We improve the rook with tempo.} 11...Re8 12.Be3 {Forced.} 12...Bg4 {This is the move that breaks through for us. 13.Bxg4 Nxg4 and we are converging on the e3-bishop. White is completely lost.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Plays 5.d4 / Study 1: White Plays 6.Nxc6 and 7.Nc3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/840e407d-54cc-45e3-9f3e-bff84a26d66d/chapters/992babe1-f1e4-4783-8154-0d1436b9c194/studies/9d88a79a-ebd8-4d7d-965d-730886798aa3/lines"]

1.e4 {This chapter is all about White defending their knight with 5.d4. In this study, we will look at how to maximize our initiative after 6.Nxc6 and 7.Nc3, and also to respond after the less common 6.Bxc4 and 6.Nc3. White begins with 1.e4. So we claim the dark squares in similar fashion.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {Challenging the dark squares. Instead of defending, we counterattack!} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White takes the material and challenges us to prove our point. Normally, we would kick out White's invading knight and then regain our pawn. But, instead we offer White a pawn in exchange for development speed and the initiative!} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White accepts the gambit, hoping for an easy advantage. Allowing White a second pawn on d5 would be too generous, so we take it with the most active piece, forcing White to do something about the knight.} 4...Qxd5 5.d4 {White plays d4, defending the knight and controlling the center. It's one of the top three choices here at 32%, so we will be seeing it quite often. We bring the knight out to challenge and pressure the center, and White has to consider that we threaten to win the pawn back. Black is winning more than half of their games after ...Nc6.} 5...Nc6 6.Bc4 {21% of the time we see this skewer, as White wants to take f7 with check! We have to let it happen, but we get the g2-pawn and weaken White's king too.} 6...Qxg2 7.Bxf7+ {White gets their check. We will move our king up and stay in contact with the bishop. This will be important later.} 7...Ke7 8.Rf1 {White saves the rook. If they play Nxc6, ...bxc6 is the only move to retain advantage. Let's remove the defender of the bishop.} 8...Nxe5 9.dxe5 {Not the only way for White to keep material roughly equal, but overwhelmingly the most popular. We will now pick up material and open up lines for our pieces.} 9...Kxf7 10.exf6 {White's position is so bad that taking back the piece is not the best move, but almost everyone does it anyway.} 10...Bg4 {We can continue to develop with incredible pressure against the white king. White's position should crumble soon, as the evaluation is -7.5.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Plays 5.d4 / Study 1: White Plays 6.Nxc6 and 7.Nc3 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/840e407d-54cc-45e3-9f3e-bff84a26d66d/chapters/992babe1-f1e4-4783-8154-0d1436b9c194/studies/9d88a79a-ebd8-4d7d-965d-730886798aa3/lines"]

1.e4 {This chapter is all about White defending their knight with 5.d4. In this study, we will look at how to maximize our initiative after 6.Nxc6 and 7.Nc3, and also to respond after the less common 6.Bxc4 and 6.Nc3. White begins with 1.e4. So we claim the dark squares in similar fashion.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {Challenging the dark squares. Instead of defending, we counterattack!} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White takes the material and challenges us to prove our point. Normally, we would kick out White's invading knight and then regain our pawn. But, instead we offer White a pawn in exchange for development speed and the initiative!} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White accepts the gambit, hoping for an easy advantage. Allowing White a second pawn on d5 would be too generous, so we take it with the most active piece, forcing White to do something about the knight.} 4...Qxd5 5.d4 {White plays d4, defending the knight and controlling the center. It's one of the top three choices here at 32%, so we will be seeing it quite often. We bring the knight out to challenge and pressure the center, and White has to consider that we threaten to win the pawn back. Black is winning more than half of their games after ...Nc6.} 5...Nc6 6.Bc4 {21% of the time we see this skewer, as White wants to take f7 with check! We have to let it happen, but we get the g2-pawn and weaken White's king too.} 6...Qxg2 7.Rf1 {White's safe option. But now there's no attack on f7.} 7...Nxe5 8.dxe5 {White recaptures, but their pawn isn't safe on e5 in the long- or short-term. Our active knight will pose some problems. We attack h2, and our knight could give a check on f3 later.} 8...Ng4 9.Bg5 {Don't get checkmated on d8!} 9...Be6 {Black has a powerful position. h2 cannot be defended, as our queen can come to e4 with check in case of Bf4 by White.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Plays 5.d4 / Study 1: White Plays 6.Nxc6 and 7.Nc3 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/840e407d-54cc-45e3-9f3e-bff84a26d66d/chapters/992babe1-f1e4-4783-8154-0d1436b9c194/studies/9d88a79a-ebd8-4d7d-965d-730886798aa3/lines"]

1.e4 {This chapter is all about White defending their knight with 5.d4. In this study, we will look at how to maximize our initiative after 6.Nxc6 and 7.Nc3, and also to respond after the less common 6.Bxc4 and 6.Nc3. White begins with 1.e4. So we claim the dark squares in similar fashion.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {Challenging the dark squares. Instead of defending, we counterattack!} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White takes the material and challenges us to prove our point. Normally, we would kick out White's invading knight and then regain our pawn. But, instead we offer White a pawn in exchange for development speed and the initiative!} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White accepts the gambit, hoping for an easy advantage. Allowing White a second pawn on d5 would be too generous, so we take it with the most active piece, forcing White to do something about the knight.} 4...Qxd5 5.d4 {White plays d4, defending the knight and controlling the center. It's one of the top three choices here at 32%, so we will be seeing it quite often. We bring the knight out to challenge and pressure the center, and White has to consider that we threaten to win the pawn back. Black is winning more than half of their games after ...Nc6.} 5...Nc6 6.Nc3 {11% of the time, White tries to run us off the board with Nc3...} 6...Bb4 {Our opponent probably just gave up the pawn on d4, but keeping queens on the board and staying active is in the spirit of our opening. We will resume play with ...Be6 and ...0-0, and will look for the right time to take on d4, as in: 7.Bd2 Qxd4.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Plays 5.d4 / Study 2: White plays 7.c4, 7.c3, 7.Be3 or 7.Qe2 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/840e407d-54cc-45e3-9f3e-bff84a26d66d/chapters/992babe1-f1e4-4783-8154-0d1436b9c194/studies/f54bd4ae-de86-4429-8e95-4c9217e8dcad/lines"]

1.e4 {In this second study in the 5.d4 chapter, we will look at a few sidelines White might also play after 6.Nxc6 Qxc6. Let's begin! "Best by test" according to Bobby Fischer is 1.e4. We will contest it with 1...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {And White challenges. We will counter-challenge and give White a decision to make. Take some material or defend?} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White takes the pawn, with some pressure on the weak f7-square too. Let's go for our gambit. More decisions for White to think about.} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White takes a second pawn, accepting the gambit. Taking with the queen gains a tempo on the knight and pressures g2 for now.} 4...Qxd5 5.d4 {White guards the knight and prepares to bring out the rest of their pieces. We will build tension in the center, not allowing White time to get comfortable.} 5...Nc6 6.Nxc6 {White tries to hold onto all their advantages by trading off the weak knight on e5 for the attacker. Taking with the queen keeps the pawn structure intact.} 6...Qxc6 7.c4 {While c4 is joint second in popularity with c3 at just 10%, they are quite different approaches to our main lines from White, and thus worth understanding. With c4, White tries to dominate the center with pawns. If we were to develop slowly or passively, White would simply be a pawn up. Our bishop check will help speed up our development.} 7...Bb4+ 8.Nc3 {Keeping the c1-bishop flexible is the only good way to defend here. Let's bring the rook to the party, while the introverted king takes the opportunity to find a corner away from the action.} 8...O-O 9.d5 {White finally deals with the pressure on g2, and with tempo. But we have a stronger threat before moving the queen, and actively tie down White's pieces with pins.} 9...Re8+ 10.Be3 {Using the correct bishop to block the file. It's time to save our queen, and we centralize on an active square.} 10...Qd6 11.Be2 {White is up a pawn and looks like they are getting everything they want, but not so fast! We threaten to liquidate all of White's advantages if they castle.} 11...Ne4 12.Rc1 {So White defends the weak c3-square. Our previous plan was stopped, but it has layers. We develop our last minor piece to the most active square. We are actually threatening to capture on c3 now.} 12...Bf5 13.O-O {And when White does castle... We launch our hidden plan.} 13...Nxc3 14.bxc3 {Of course White wants to keep the rook...} 14...Ba3 {But the rook is trapped! If Ra1, then ...Bb2. Black only has a minimal edge here, since even if we win the exchange, White would have the bishop pair and a strong extra central pawn. But we put a lot of pressure on White and lost nothing, so that counts as a success. If White pushes c5, we can either give up winning the exchange to neutralize their strength, or play ...Qg6.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Plays 5.d4 / Study 2: White plays 7.c4, 7.c3, 7.Be3 or 7.Qe2 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/840e407d-54cc-45e3-9f3e-bff84a26d66d/chapters/992babe1-f1e4-4783-8154-0d1436b9c194/studies/f54bd4ae-de86-4429-8e95-4c9217e8dcad/lines"]

1.e4 {In this second study in the 5.d4 chapter, we will look at a few sidelines White might also play after 6.Nxc6 Qxc6. Let's begin! "Best by test" according to Bobby Fischer is 1.e4. We will contest it with 1...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {And White challenges. We will counter-challenge and give White a decision to make. Take some material or defend?} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White takes the pawn, with some pressure on the weak f7-square too. Let's go for our gambit. More decisions for White to think about.} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White takes a second pawn, accepting the gambit. Taking with the queen gains a tempo on the knight and pressures g2 for now.} 4...Qxd5 5.d4 {White guards the knight and prepares to bring out the rest of their pieces. We will build tension in the center, not allowing White time to get comfortable.} 5...Nc6 6.Nxc6 {White tries to hold onto all their advantages by trading off the weak knight on e5 for the attacker. Taking with the queen keeps the pawn structure intact.} 6...Qxc6 7.c4 {While c4 is joint second in popularity with c3 at just 10%, they are quite different approaches to our main lines from White, and thus worth understanding. With c4, White tries to dominate the center with pawns. If we were to develop slowly or passively, White would simply be a pawn up. Our bishop check will help speed up our development.} 7...Bb4+ 8.Nc3 {Keeping the c1-bishop flexible is the only good way to defend here. Let's bring the rook to the party, while the introverted king takes the opportunity to find a corner away from the action.} 8...O-O 9.d5 {White finally deals with the pressure on g2, and with tempo. But we have a stronger threat before moving the queen, and actively tie down White's pieces with pins.} 9...Re8+ 10.Be2 {White may think that they can move the f1-bishop now that the attack on the g2-pawn is blocked... But that would be a mistake. White hung c4, and we can remove the defender of e2 and weaken the pawn structure.} 10...Bxc3+ 11.bxc3 {White has taken back immediately in every game, but it doesn't help with c4 undefended. Kf1 would have been a better choice!} 11...Qxc4 {So we take the pawn. The bishop on e2 is loose, the pawns on d5 and c3 are hanging, and White's king is unable to castle. Although material is equal, and White has the bishop pair, Black is much better, with the evaluation showing -4.6. Black should continue to play actively against White's king to make the most of the position while picking up the odd pawn if possible.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Plays 5.d4 / Study 2: White plays 7.c4, 7.c3, 7.Be3 or 7.Qe2 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/840e407d-54cc-45e3-9f3e-bff84a26d66d/chapters/992babe1-f1e4-4783-8154-0d1436b9c194/studies/f54bd4ae-de86-4429-8e95-4c9217e8dcad/lines"]

1.e4 {In this second study in the 5.d4 chapter, we will look at a few sidelines White might also play after 6.Nxc6 Qxc6. Let's begin! "Best by test" according to Bobby Fischer is 1.e4. We will contest it with 1...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {And White challenges. We will counter-challenge and give White a decision to make. Take some material or defend?} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White takes the pawn, with some pressure on the weak f7-square too. Let's go for our gambit. More decisions for White to think about.} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White takes a second pawn, accepting the gambit. Taking with the queen gains a tempo on the knight and pressures g2 for now.} 4...Qxd5 5.d4 {White guards the knight and prepares to bring out the rest of their pieces. We will build tension in the center, not allowing White time to get comfortable.} 5...Nc6 6.Nxc6 {White tries to hold onto all their advantages by trading off the weak knight on e5 for the attacker. Taking with the queen keeps the pawn structure intact.} 6...Qxc6 7.c4 {While c4 is joint second in popularity with c3 at just 10%, they are quite different approaches to our main lines from White, and thus worth understanding. With c4, White tries to dominate the center with pawns. If we were to develop slowly or passively, White would simply be a pawn up. Our bishop check will help speed up our development.} 7...Bb4+ 8.Bd2 {What seems like a natural move, counterattacking the undefended bishop on b4, is a mistake! It looks like we are hanging our bishop, but the White king is critically weak, and their dark-squared bishop was an important defender.} 8...O-O 9.Bxb4 {We are now down a bishop and a pawn, but that is only part of the story. We are ready to bring all of our pieces into the attack. It's time to bring the pain to White. They have three ways to defend, and none of them are good.} 9...Re8+ 10.Be2 {Let's look at the optimistic attempt first. White blocks with the bishop. But that left the g2-pawn hanging! And now we are attacking the rook.} 10...Qxg2 11.Rf1 {White saves the rook, but their king will never feel safe again. ...Bg4 works because we first removed the g2-pawn. If White plays f3 now we can just take the pawn.} 11...Bg4 12.Nc3 {Bringing the inactive knight out to defend the bishop. We sacrifice the rook! We want to bring the other rook to e8 and we don't give White any time to fix anything this way.} 12...Rxe2+ 13.Nxe2 {The only sane move.} 13...Re8 {White has no way to save the knight here. Since Black's king is safe and our pieces are active and coordinated, while the opposite is true for White, Black is clearly better. The evaluation is -4.2. Although piece trades will happen, we don't necessarily need to force them as all the pressure is on the White king.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Plays 5.d4 / Study 2: White plays 7.c4, 7.c3, 7.Be3 or 7.Qe2 / Variation 4"]
