1.e4 {In this second study in the 5.d4 chapter, we will look at a few sidelines White might also play after 6.Nxc6 Qxc6. Let's begin! "Best by test" according to Bobby Fischer is 1.e4. We will contest it with 1...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {And White challenges. We will counter-challenge and give White a decision to make. Take some material or defend?} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White takes the pawn, with some pressure on the weak f7-square too. Let's go for our gambit. More decisions for White to think about.} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White takes a second pawn, accepting the gambit. Taking with the queen gains a tempo on the knight and pressures g2 for now.} 4...Qxd5 5.d4 {White guards the knight and prepares to bring out the rest of their pieces. We will build tension in the center, not allowing White time to get comfortable.} 5...Nc6 6.Nxc6 {White tries to hold onto all their advantages by trading off the weak knight on e5 for the attacker. Taking with the queen keeps the pawn structure intact.} 6...Qxc6 7.c4 {While c4 is joint second in popularity with c3 at just 10%, they are quite different approaches to our main lines from White, and thus worth understanding. With c4, White tries to dominate the center with pawns. If we were to develop slowly or passively, White would simply be a pawn up. Our bishop check will help speed up our development.} 7...Bb4+ 8.Bd2 {What seems like a natural move, counterattacking the undefended bishop on b4, is a mistake! It looks like we are hanging our bishop, but the White king is critically weak, and their dark-squared bishop was an important defender.} 8...O-O 9.Bxb4 {We are now down a bishop and a pawn, but that is only part of the story. We are ready to bring all of our pieces into the attack. It's time to bring the pain to White. They have three ways to defend, and none of them are good.} 9...Re8+ 10.Kd2 {Let's have a quick look at Kd2 trying to hide on the queenside.} 10...Ne4+ {Black will equalize material at the very least, with a safer king and better pieces. The evaluation is -3. Trading off White's biggest asset, the bishop pair, would further emphasize the advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Plays 5.d4 / Study 2: White plays 7.c4, 7.c3, 7.Be3 or 7.Qe2 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/840e407d-54cc-45e3-9f3e-bff84a26d66d/chapters/992babe1-f1e4-4783-8154-0d1436b9c194/studies/f54bd4ae-de86-4429-8e95-4c9217e8dcad/lines"]

1.e4 {In this second study in the 5.d4 chapter, we will look at a few sidelines White might also play after 6.Nxc6 Qxc6. Let's begin! "Best by test" according to Bobby Fischer is 1.e4. We will contest it with 1...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {And White challenges. We will counter-challenge and give White a decision to make. Take some material or defend?} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White takes the pawn, with some pressure on the weak f7-square too. Let's go for our gambit. More decisions for White to think about.} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White takes a second pawn, accepting the gambit. Taking with the queen gains a tempo on the knight and pressures g2 for now.} 4...Qxd5 5.d4 {White guards the knight and prepares to bring out the rest of their pieces. We will build tension in the center, not allowing White time to get comfortable.} 5...Nc6 6.Nxc6 {White tries to hold onto all their advantages by trading off the weak knight on e5 for the attacker. Taking with the queen keeps the pawn structure intact.} 6...Qxc6 7.c3 {This is obviously an attempt to be solid and unambitious, just holding onto the pawn, and 10% of games go this way.} 7...Bd6 {Black's lead in development lets us retain the initiative for the pawn, and Black is scoring a very respectable 57% win rate here. The consistent theme in this position is control of the e-file and weakening White's dark squares with moves like ...Bg4, provoking f3, ...Nd5 or ...Ng4 to trade the bishop on e3, or ...c5 to break White's chain in the future. We could also consider trading off White's better light-square bishop, but our specific plans must be adjusted based on what White chooses to do.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Plays 5.d4 / Study 2: White plays 7.c4, 7.c3, 7.Be3 or 7.Qe2 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/840e407d-54cc-45e3-9f3e-bff84a26d66d/chapters/992babe1-f1e4-4783-8154-0d1436b9c194/studies/f54bd4ae-de86-4429-8e95-4c9217e8dcad/lines"]

1.e4 {In this second study in the 5.d4 chapter, we will look at a few sidelines White might also play after 6.Nxc6 Qxc6. Let's begin! "Best by test" according to Bobby Fischer is 1.e4. We will contest it with 1...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {And White challenges. We will counter-challenge and give White a decision to make. Take some material or defend?} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White takes the pawn, with some pressure on the weak f7-square too. Let's go for our gambit. More decisions for White to think about.} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White takes a second pawn, accepting the gambit. Taking with the queen gains a tempo on the knight and pressures g2 for now.} 4...Qxd5 5.d4 {White guards the knight and prepares to bring out the rest of their pieces. We will build tension in the center, not allowing White time to get comfortable.} 5...Nc6 6.Nxc6 {White tries to hold onto all their advantages by trading off the weak knight on e5 for the attacker. Taking with the queen keeps the pawn structure intact.} 6...Qxc6 7.Be3 {In most of our lines, White is actively fighting for control of the light squares, and has major trouble with the e-file in particular. Here, White tries to fix the e-file problem first. It takes a 5% share of games from here, with Black still winning 53%. So, naturally, we take the light squares and develop a bishop.} 7...Bf5 8.Nc3 {Half of the time White brings the knight out. We were threatening to win our pawn back on c2 and leave White with a weak pawn on d4.} 8...Bb4 {You should be getting used to the pin idea by now. Black is winning 64% of games here. Objectively the engine calls it equal, but practically it is just difficult for White to do anything here and we will regain our pawn. We will look to centralize the knight on f6. We are also flexible to castle on either side of the board.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: White Plays 5.d4 / Study 2: White plays 7.c4, 7.c3, 7.Be3 or 7.Qe2 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/840e407d-54cc-45e3-9f3e-bff84a26d66d/chapters/992babe1-f1e4-4783-8154-0d1436b9c194/studies/f54bd4ae-de86-4429-8e95-4c9217e8dcad/lines"]

1.e4 {In this second study in the 5.d4 chapter, we will look at a few sidelines White might also play after 6.Nxc6 Qxc6. Let's begin! "Best by test" according to Bobby Fischer is 1.e4. We will contest it with 1...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {And White challenges. We will counter-challenge and give White a decision to make. Take some material or defend?} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White takes the pawn, with some pressure on the weak f7-square too. Let's go for our gambit. More decisions for White to think about.} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White takes a second pawn, accepting the gambit. Taking with the queen gains a tempo on the knight and pressures g2 for now.} 4...Qxd5 5.d4 {White guards the knight and prepares to bring out the rest of their pieces. We will build tension in the center, not allowing White time to get comfortable.} 5...Nc6 6.Nxc6 {White tries to hold onto all their advantages by trading off the weak knight on e5 for the attacker. Taking with the queen keeps the pawn structure intact.} 6...Qxc6 7.Qe2+ {And finally, for this study, let's look at the most aggressive responses. 7.a4 with the threat of Bb5 can be neutralized by ...a6, so we can set that aside. Qe2 is down to 4% of games from here, and White is doing much better, losing half of their games and winning 44%... but still good for us! This odd-looking move is once again about using the e-file, without delay!} 7...Kd8 8.Nc3 {White controls important light squares and prepares to castle queenside. And of course we should pin the knight! We will create threats on both the e-file and c3, and one dark-squared bishop can't cover both.} 8...Bb4 9.Qc4 {White tries to force us to trade down with nothing for our pawn. Before we deal with that, let's give a check and grab the e-file.} 9...Re8+ 10.Be3 {Of course, White had a plan and is about to castle. But not so fast! We have this sneaky move using the pin on the knight and defending the bishop. 10...Nd5 will complicate things again, although in reality, should White castle, we are mainly just threatening to win our pawn back on c3 and play on with equal material. ...Nxe3 is also playable.} 10...Nd5 11.Qxc6 {White should just trade the queens off. Forced.} 11...bxc6 12.Kd2 {White moves the king to d2, and the two weakest squares in White's camp are defended. We aren't without resources, though. We fight for the light squares that White can't control.} 12...Be6 13.a3 {White will probably want to unpin their knight at some point, so they attack our bishop. And if so, we just take.} 13...Bxc3+ 14.bxc3 {White recaptures with the pawn.} 14...Nb6 {See how we have locked down outposts in front of the white king? Even though White is better here according to the engine, we can still force concessions and play a good game, as White's advantage is mostly just material.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White Plays 5.Qe2 / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/840e407d-54cc-45e3-9f3e-bff84a26d66d/chapters/60dcf772-d0d0-433c-96c5-68e7b30da15e/studies/f2274905-b163-4c37-a33e-26fbc042c80d/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the study of Chapter 3 in the Petroff Gotham Gambit. In this one, we’ll explore what happens after 5.Qe2, which is White’s strongest reply against our gambit. White opens with the most popular first move, staking a claim in the center. We respond symmetrically.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops a knight and immediately targets our e5 pawn. We go for the Petroff Defense, a mirrored setup that challenges e4 right away instead of defending e5.} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White captures the pawn on e5. And now we’re in the Petroff Gotham Gambit!} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White accepts the gambit. We recapture with the queen, activating it and putting pressure on the knight.} 4...Qxd5 5.Qe2 {This is White’s best response to the gambit, as mentioned in the introduction, though it only shows up in 22% of games. It threatens a discovered check and forces us to be precise. We acknowledge the threat and develop our dark-squared bishop, blocking the queen’s vision along the e-file.} 5...Be7 6.Nc3 {White develops while gaining a tempo on our queen. It's the best and most popular move here, played in 64% of games. We go back to d8. We sidestep any Qb5+ ideas that could force a queen trade. The upside? White’s queen is awkwardly placed on e2, and we’ll soon generate activity. In nearly every variation except one, we’ll have clear compensation for the pawn.} 6...Qd8 7.d4 {This is the second most played move, played in 19% of games, but it simply hangs a pawn! We gladly take the pawn back. Material is now equal.} 7...Qxd4 8.Nb5 {White jumps in with the knight, forking our queen and the c7-pawn, which would also fork our king and rook. We retreat the queen to its original square, covering c7.} 8...Qd8 9.Bf4 {White develops the bishop and x-rays the c7-pawn. If the e5-knight ever moves, c7 will be attacked twice. So we push ...c6, kicking the knight back and preventing tactical problems on c7. Our plan is simple: castle short for safety, and if White brings a rook to the d-file, our queen will escape to a5. But let's also see a line that looks dangerous, yet gives us an advantage.} 9...c6 10.Nxc6 {White sacrifices the knight on c6 to open the bishop’s diagonal and support the idea of jumping into c7 with check. We recapture with our knight.} 10...Nxc6 11.Nc7+ {Here it comes: 11.Nc7+ forks our king and rook. At first glance, it looks like White will win material and keep the knight safe, but… We calmly slide the king to f8.} 11...Kf8 12.Nxa8 {White grabs the rook.} 12...Bf5 {We immediately target the knight. If White tries to retreat with 13.Nc7, then 13...Nd4 is crushing, forking the queen and the c2 pawn. Black is already better.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White Plays 5.Qe2 / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/840e407d-54cc-45e3-9f3e-bff84a26d66d/chapters/60dcf772-d0d0-433c-96c5-68e7b30da15e/studies/f2274905-b163-4c37-a33e-26fbc042c80d/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the study of Chapter 3 in the Petroff Gotham Gambit. In this one, we’ll explore what happens after 5.Qe2, which is White’s strongest reply against our gambit. White opens with the most popular first move, staking a claim in the center. We respond symmetrically.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops a knight and immediately targets our e5 pawn. We go for the Petroff Defense, a mirrored setup that challenges e4 right away instead of defending e5.} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White captures the pawn on e5. And now we’re in the Petroff Gotham Gambit!} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White accepts the gambit. We recapture with the queen, activating it and putting pressure on the knight.} 4...Qxd5 5.Qe2 {This is White’s best response to the gambit, as mentioned in the introduction, though it only shows up in 22% of games. It threatens a discovered check and forces us to be precise. We acknowledge the threat and develop our dark-squared bishop, blocking the queen’s vision along the e-file.} 5...Be7 6.Qb5+ {White can also go for the immediate queen trade. It’s the second-best option for White, but it only shows up in about 6% of games. We block with the knight! It’s important not to trade queens ourselves, as we don’t want to speed up White’s development.} 6...Nbd7 7.Qxd5 {White gets the queen trade they were looking for. We recapture with the knight.} 7...Nxd5 8.Nxd7 {White keeps simplifying with another trade.} 8...Bxd7 {We take back with the bishop. White is objectively better here since they're still up a pawn, but we have full development, while all of White's pieces remain on the back rank. Our plan is to castle long, play ...Bc6, and look for active knight jumps like ...Nf4 or ...Nb4.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White Plays 5.Qe2 / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/840e407d-54cc-45e3-9f3e-bff84a26d66d/chapters/60dcf772-d0d0-433c-96c5-68e7b30da15e/studies/f2274905-b163-4c37-a33e-26fbc042c80d/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the study of Chapter 3 in the Petroff Gotham Gambit. In this one, we’ll explore what happens after 5.Qe2, which is White’s strongest reply against our gambit. White opens with the most popular first move, staking a claim in the center. We respond symmetrically.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops a knight and immediately targets our e5 pawn. We go for the Petroff Defense, a mirrored setup that challenges e4 right away instead of defending e5.} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White captures the pawn on e5. And now we’re in the Petroff Gotham Gambit!} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White accepts the gambit. We recapture with the queen, activating it and putting pressure on the knight.} 4...Qxd5 5.Qe2 {This is White’s best response to the gambit, as mentioned in the introduction, though it only shows up in 22% of games. It threatens a discovered check and forces us to be precise. We acknowledge the threat and develop our dark-squared bishop, blocking the queen’s vision along the e-file.} 5...Be7 6.Nc3 {White develops while gaining a tempo on our queen. It's the best and most popular move here, played in 64% of games. We go back to d8. We sidestep any Qb5+ ideas that could force a queen trade. The upside? White’s queen is awkwardly placed on e2, and we’ll soon generate activity. In nearly every variation except one, we’ll have clear compensation for the pawn.} 6...Qd8 7.b3 {This is the best option for White and the only line that keeps the +1 advantage. It has appeared in just 3% of amateur games, but was played in the only known master-level encounter. The idea is to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop along the long diagonal and prepare long castling. We castle short. Since White will likely castle long, we're aiming to create imbalances.} 7...O-O 8.Bb2 {White fianchettos their bishop, preparing to castle long. They’re catching up in development with a safe king. We develop our knight, inviting the trade. We actually want White to double our pawns, as it opens lines on the queenside. Other ideas included ...Bc5–Bd4 to challenge the fianchettoed bishop, or simply pushing our queenside pawns with ...a5.} 8...Nc6 9.Nxc6 {White captures our knight, damaging our structure, but opening the b-file in the process. We recapture.} 9...bxc6 10.O-O-O {White castles long and achieves a safe king with well-placed pieces.} 10...a5 {We start pushing the a-pawn, aiming to open lines against the enemy king. White's queen is still blocking their bishop in. The idea of ...Bc5–Bd4 was also available to counter the bishop on b2. That said, White holds a clear advantage. The engine gives +1.7, but this line is rare, and still presents White with many practical problems.} *

