[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/840e407d-54cc-45e3-9f3e-bff84a26d66d/chapters/60dcf772-d0d0-433c-96c5-68e7b30da15e/studies/f2274905-b163-4c37-a33e-26fbc042c80d/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the study of Chapter 3 in the Petroff Gotham Gambit. In this one, we’ll explore what happens after 5.Qe2, which is White’s strongest reply against our gambit. White opens with the most popular first move, staking a claim in the center. We respond symmetrically.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops a knight and immediately targets our e5 pawn. We go for the Petroff Defense, a mirrored setup that challenges e4 right away instead of defending e5.} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White captures the pawn on e5. And now we’re in the Petroff Gotham Gambit!} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White accepts the gambit. We recapture with the queen, activating it and putting pressure on the knight.} 4...Qxd5 5.Qe2 {This is White’s best response to the gambit, as mentioned in the introduction, though it only shows up in 22% of games. It threatens a discovered check and forces us to be precise. We acknowledge the threat and develop our dark-squared bishop, blocking the queen’s vision along the e-file.} 5...Be7 6.c4 {White pushes the c-pawn to hit our queen and prepare a strong center. This is the second most played move at the amateur level, but it’s already inaccurate. Black has equalized. White players choose this move 13% of the time. We sidestep to a5, keeping the queen active and pinning the d-pawn to the king.} 6...Qa5 7.Nc3 {White develops and breaks the pin, now preparing d4 to claim the center. We castle short. Our plan is simple: bring the rook to e8, line it up with the queen, and develop the knight to c6.} 7...O-O 8.d4 {White gains central space, but now the c3-knight is pinned to the king. We improve our bishop and add pressure to the pinned knight on c3.} 8...Bb4 9.Bd2 {White breaks the pin by developing the bishop.} 9...Re8 {And now we pin the other knight, this time to the queen! Thanks to our better piece activity and the awkward placement of White’s queen, we’re already pressing for an advantage. Our next move will likely be ...Nc6. If White ever moves the queen off the e-file, ...c5 becomes very strong, should we have tempo to achieve it.} *

