1.e4 {Welcome to the study of Chapter 3 in the Petroff Gotham Gambit. In this one, we’ll explore what happens after 5.Qe2, which is White’s strongest reply against our gambit. White opens with the most popular first move, staking a claim in the center. We respond symmetrically.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops a knight and immediately targets our e5 pawn. We go for the Petroff Defense, a mirrored setup that challenges e4 right away instead of defending e5.} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White captures the pawn on e5. And now we’re in the Petroff Gotham Gambit!} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White accepts the gambit. We recapture with the queen, activating it and putting pressure on the knight.} 4...Qxd5 5.Qe2 {This is White’s best response to the gambit, as mentioned in the introduction, though it only shows up in 22% of games. It threatens a discovered check and forces us to be precise. We acknowledge the threat and develop our dark-squared bishop, blocking the queen’s vision along the e-file.} 5...Be7 6.Nc3 {White develops while gaining a tempo on our queen. It's the best and most popular move here, played in 64% of games. We go back to d8. We sidestep any Qb5+ ideas that could force a queen trade. The upside? White’s queen is awkwardly placed on e2, and we’ll soon generate activity. In nearly every variation except one, we’ll have clear compensation for the pawn.} 6...Qd8 7.d3 {White opens their dark-squared bishop. At the amateur level, this move is seen about 34% of the time. And already, Black is nearly equal here.} 7...O-O {We castle short. Our ideas are clear: place the rook on the e-file, pin the knight on c3, and develop with ...Nc6.} *

