1.e4 {Welcome to the study of Chapter 3 in the Petroff Gotham Gambit. In this one, we’ll explore what happens after 5.Qe2, which is White’s strongest reply against our gambit. White opens with the most popular first move, staking a claim in the center. We respond symmetrically.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops a knight and immediately targets our e5 pawn. We go for the Petroff Defense, a mirrored setup that challenges e4 right away instead of defending e5.} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White captures the pawn on e5. And now we’re in the Petroff Gotham Gambit!} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White accepts the gambit. We recapture with the queen, activating it and putting pressure on the knight.} 4...Qxd5 5.Qe2 {This is White’s best response to the gambit, as mentioned in the introduction, though it only shows up in 22% of games. It threatens a discovered check and forces us to be precise. We acknowledge the threat and develop our dark-squared bishop, blocking the queen’s vision along the e-file.} 5...Be7 6.Nc3 {White develops while gaining a tempo on our queen. It's the best and most popular move here, played in 64% of games. We go back to d8. We sidestep any Qb5+ ideas that could force a queen trade. The upside? White’s queen is awkwardly placed on e2, and we’ll soon generate activity. In nearly every variation except one, we’ll have clear compensation for the pawn.} 6...Qd8 7.g3 {White prepares to fianchetto the light-squared bishop and aims to castle short. This setup is played in 13% of games, but it gives Black an easy path to equalize. We castle short.} 7...O-O 8.Bg2 {White completes the fianchetto, developing the bishop toward the long diagonal. We place our rook on the open e-file, lining it up directly with White’s queen.} 8...Re8 9.O-O {White brings their king to safety.} 9...Bc5 {And now comes our idea! By playing ...Bc5, we threaten ...Bd4, which would win a piece. White’s best option is to play d4, giving up a pawn in order to allow their dark-squared bishop to defend the e5-knight.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 4/5 Sidelines / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/840e407d-54cc-45e3-9f3e-bff84a26d66d/chapters/e1cf55ea-1597-4965-8bd8-96cac6f51add/studies/97f323ac-0bce-4c2b-b02c-101938da5cee/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 4 of the Petroff Gotham Gambit. In this study, we’ll look at the sidelines White might play on move 4 and on move 5. These lines are rare — but it’s important to be prepared for them. White starts the game with e4. We start the game the same way, replying with e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White brings out the kingside knight, putting pressure on our e5 pawn right away. We choose the Petroff Defense, counterattacking e4 instead of defending our e5 pawn.} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White grabs the e5 pawn with their knight. Let's enter the Gotham Petroff Gambit with 3...d5!} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White takes on d5 in the main line. We take back with the queen, centralizing it and applying pressure to the knight.} 4...Qxd5 5.Nd3 {White retreats the knight to d3. A rare move, played in just 2% of games. It blocks the d-pawn and hinders the development of the dark-squared bishop, making it a dubious choice. We respond with natural development, bringing out our knight.} 5...Nc6 6.Nc3 {White develops their other knight and gains a tempo by hitting our queen. We keep the queen active by staying on the fifth rank.} 6...Qa5 7.Be2 {The knight on d3 is so awkwardly placed that the engine already suggests moving it again. Instead of addressing the misplaced piece, White develops the light-squared bishop and prepares to castle short. We jump forward with the knight, improving the piece and threatening to win the bishop pair, even if it’s not immediately possible. Another option was ...Bd6, an equally strong move, but ours is the less common choice.} 7...Nd4 8.O-O {White gets the king to safety.} 8...Bf5 {We continue with smooth development, preparing to castle long. We would also like the b4 square for our bishop, adding pressure to c3 (if the knight moves from d3). Even though we’re still a pawn down, all of our pieces coordinate perfectly, and White’s d3-knight is still in the way. We will first play ...0-0-0 and then consider ...Nxe2.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 4/5 Sidelines / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/840e407d-54cc-45e3-9f3e-bff84a26d66d/chapters/e1cf55ea-1597-4965-8bd8-96cac6f51add/studies/97f323ac-0bce-4c2b-b02c-101938da5cee/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 4 of the Petroff Gotham Gambit. In this study, we’ll look at the sidelines White might play on move 4 and on move 5. These lines are rare — but it’s important to be prepared for them. White starts the game with e4. We start the game the same way, replying with e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White brings out the kingside knight, putting pressure on our e5 pawn right away. We choose the Petroff Defense, counterattacking e4 instead of defending our e5 pawn.} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White grabs the e5 pawn with their knight. Let's enter the Gotham Petroff Gambit with 3...d5!} 3...d5 4.d4 {White declines the gambit and instead plays 4.d4, reinforcing the knight and claiming more space in the center. This move invites us into a line of the mainline Petroff and is seen in about 13% of games. We capture on e4, fully transposing into a line of the real Petroff. However, this exact position is incredibly rare due to our Gotham Gambit move order.} 4...Nxe4 5.Bd3 {White develops the light-squared bishop. It now looks directly at our knight on e4. We respond by developing our knight. The idea is to trade off White’s well-placed knight without allowing doubled c-pawns.} 5...Nd7 6.Nxf7 {White sacrifices the knight, but there’s no real danger. Without a strong follow-up, this isn’t something we need to fear. We calmly accept the sacrifice.} 6...Kxf7 7.Qh5+ {White brings the queen into the attack, but it’s not as threatening as it looks. Playing 7...g6 would be a mistake. It allows Qxd5, losing the central pawn for no reason.} 7...Ke6 {The king steps up to defend the d5-pawn. The position is equal. Our next move will be ...Ndf6, reinforcing our center. White may try to force a perpetual check, but if they go for a passive move like 0-0, we’ll already be better.} *

