1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 4 of the Petroff Gotham Gambit. In this study, we’ll look at the sidelines White might play on move 4 and on move 5. These lines are rare — but it’s important to be prepared for them. White starts the game with e4. We start the game the same way, replying with e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White brings out the kingside knight, putting pressure on our e5 pawn right away. We choose the Petroff Defense, counterattacking e4 instead of defending our e5 pawn.} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White grabs the e5 pawn with their knight. Let's enter the Gotham Petroff Gambit with 3...d5!} 3...d5 4.d4 {White declines the gambit and instead plays 4.d4, reinforcing the knight and claiming more space in the center. This move invites us into a line of the mainline Petroff and is seen in about 13% of games. We capture on e4, fully transposing into a line of the real Petroff. However, this exact position is incredibly rare due to our Gotham Gambit move order.} 4...Nxe4 5.Bd3 {White develops the light-squared bishop. It now looks directly at our knight on e4. We respond by developing our knight. The idea is to trade off White’s well-placed knight without allowing doubled c-pawns.} 5...Nd7 6.O-O {White chooses to castle short. We respond by trading knights.} 6...Nxe5 7.dxe5 {White recaptures. We retreat the knight to c5, targeting the light-squared bishop.} 7...Nc5 8.Be2 {White retreats the bishop, preserving the bishop pair.} 8...c6 {We solidify our center with ...c6. Our next steps will be to finish our development with ...Be7, castle short, and likely place our light-squared bishop on f5.} *

