1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 4 of the Petroff Gotham Gambit. In this study, we’ll look at the sidelines White might play on move 4 and on move 5. These lines are rare — but it’s important to be prepared for them. White starts the game with e4. We start the game the same way, replying with e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White brings out the kingside knight, putting pressure on our e5 pawn right away. We choose the Petroff Defense, counterattacking e4 instead of defending our e5 pawn.} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White grabs the e5 pawn with their knight. Let's enter the Gotham Petroff Gambit with 3...d5!} 3...d5 4.d4 {White declines the gambit and instead plays 4.d4, reinforcing the knight and claiming more space in the center. This move invites us into a line of the mainline Petroff and is seen in about 13% of games. We capture on e4, fully transposing into a line of the real Petroff. However, this exact position is incredibly rare due to our Gotham Gambit move order.} 4...Nxe4 5.Bd3 {White develops the light-squared bishop. It now looks directly at our knight on e4. We respond by developing our knight. The idea is to trade off White’s well-placed knight without allowing doubled c-pawns.} 5...Nd7 6.Nxd7 {White trades knights. We recapture with the bishop.} 6...Bxd7 7.O-O {White castles short. We go for 7...Qh4 — only played 8% of the time, but with excellent results for Black. The idea? Castle long, aim for opposite-side castling, and keep the aggressive spirit of the Gotham Gambit alive.} 7...Qh4 8.c4 {White challenges our center with c4, the best move in the position.} 8...O-O-O {We castle long and look to develop our bishop to d6. If White tries to prevent that with c5, we’ll change plans and look to fianchetto the bishop with ...g6 and ...Bg7.} *

