1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 4 of the Petroff Gotham Gambit. In this study, we’ll look at the sidelines White might play on move 4 and on move 5. These lines are rare — but it’s important to be prepared for them. White starts the game with e4. We start the game the same way, replying with e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White brings out the kingside knight, putting pressure on our e5 pawn right away. We choose the Petroff Defense, counterattacking e4 instead of defending our e5 pawn.} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White grabs the e5 pawn with their knight. Let's enter the Gotham Petroff Gambit with 3...d5!} 3...d5 4.d4 {White declines the gambit and instead plays 4.d4, reinforcing the knight and claiming more space in the center. This move invites us into a line of the mainline Petroff and is seen in about 13% of games. We capture on e4, fully transposing into a line of the real Petroff. However, this exact position is incredibly rare due to our Gotham Gambit move order.} 4...Nxe4 5.Bd3 {White develops the light-squared bishop. It now looks directly at our knight on e4. We respond by developing our knight. The idea is to trade off White’s well-placed knight without allowing doubled c-pawns.} 5...Nd7 6.Nxd7 {White trades knights. We recapture with the bishop.} 6...Bxd7 7.O-O {White castles short. We go for 7...Qh4 — only played 8% of the time, but with excellent results for Black. The idea? Castle long, aim for opposite-side castling, and keep the aggressive spirit of the Gotham Gambit alive.} 7...Qh4 8.g3 {White’s second most popular option is to kick our queen with g3.} 8...Qf6 {We calmly retreat to f3, where the queen keeps pressure on the d4 pawn. White has slightly weakened their kingside, giving us a potential pawn hook. A natural plan from here is to castle long, play ...Bh3, and launch a kingside attack with ...h5–h4!} *

