1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 4 of the Petroff Gotham Gambit. In this study, we’ll look at the sidelines White might play on move 4 and on move 5. These lines are rare — but it’s important to be prepared for them. White starts the game with e4. We start the game the same way, replying with e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White brings out the kingside knight, putting pressure on our e5 pawn right away. We choose the Petroff Defense, counterattacking e4 instead of defending our e5 pawn.} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White grabs the e5 pawn with their knight. Let's enter the Gotham Petroff Gambit with 3...d5!} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White takes on d5 in the main line. We take back with the queen, centralizing it and applying pressure to the knight.} 4...Qxd5 5.Nc4 {White can also play 5.Nc4, a move seen in 5% of master games, but less than 1% at amateur level! It’s one of the best options in the position, aiming to reroute the knight to e3 and gain a tempo on our queen. We develop our light-squared bishop.} 5...Be6 6.Nc3 {Alternatively, White can develop the queenside knight to c3, again hitting our queen and gaining tempo.} 6...Qd4 {We keep the queen active and advance it to d4. The idea is to develop with ...Nc6, castle long, and bring our dark-squared bishop to d6. If White plays 8.Nb5, we’ll need to retreat with ...Qd8 to defend both c7 and the a8-rook.} *

