1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 4 of the Petroff Gotham Gambit. In this study, we’ll look at the sidelines White might play on move 4 and on move 5. These lines are rare — but it’s important to be prepared for them. White starts the game with e4. We start the game the same way, replying with e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White brings out the kingside knight, putting pressure on our e5 pawn right away. We choose the Petroff Defense, counterattacking e4 instead of defending our e5 pawn.} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White grabs the e5 pawn with their knight. Let's enter the Gotham Petroff Gambit with 3...d5!} 3...d5 4.exd5 {White takes on d5 in the main line. We take back with the queen, centralizing it and applying pressure to the knight.} 4...Qxd5 5.f4 {White tries to anchor the knight on e5 with the f-pawn — a rare idea, seen in only 1% of games, but considered the 5th best move in the position. We respond with natural development. By bringing out our dark-squared bishop, we control the long g1–a7 diagonal, making it harder for White to castle short.} 5...Bc5 6.Nc3 {As in many lines, White gains a tempo by developing the knight to c3, hitting our queen. We push the queen forward, creating a mating threat on f2. Surprisingly, in 12% of games, White misses this threat and gets mated.} 6...Qd4 7.Qe2 {White defends f2 with the queen.} 7...O-O {We castle short. From here, our plan is clear: bring the rook to e8, line it up with the queen, and develop with ...Nc6 — the light-squared bishop will likely go to f5. If White plays 8.Nb5, we’ll need to drop the queen back to d8, covering c7 and keeping the a8-rook safe.} *

