1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 4 of the Petroff Gotham Gambit. In this study, we’ll look at the sidelines White might play on move 4 and on move 5. These lines are rare — but it’s important to be prepared for them. White starts the game with e4. We start the game the same way, replying with e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White brings out the kingside knight, putting pressure on our e5 pawn right away. We choose the Petroff Defense, counterattacking e4 instead of defending our e5 pawn.} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White grabs the e5 pawn with their knight. Let's enter the Gotham Petroff Gambit with 3...d5!} 3...d5 4.d3 {White declines the gambit and reinforces the e4-pawn. A rare move, seen in only 5% of games. We don’t rush to capture. Instead, we develop while targeting the knight on e5, forcing it to move.} 4...Bd6 5.Nf3 {The knight steps back to safety. Now that the knight has retreated, we capture in the center.} 5...dxe4 6.dxe4 {White recaptures the pawn. We take with the knight and already have more pieces developed than White.} 6...Nxe4 7.Bd3 {White develops the bishop and looks to gain a tempo by hitting our knight.} 7...Nc5 {But we simply jump to c5, targeting the newly developed bishop and aiming to win the bishop pair. Our next plans: castle short, take on d3 if allowed, and finish development with ...Nc6 and ...Bf5.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 4/5 Sidelines / Study 1: All Lines / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/840e407d-54cc-45e3-9f3e-bff84a26d66d/chapters/e1cf55ea-1597-4965-8bd8-96cac6f51add/studies/97f323ac-0bce-4c2b-b02c-101938da5cee/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to Chapter 4 of the Petroff Gotham Gambit. In this study, we’ll look at the sidelines White might play on move 4 and on move 5. These lines are rare — but it’s important to be prepared for them. White starts the game with e4. We start the game the same way, replying with e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White brings out the kingside knight, putting pressure on our e5 pawn right away. We choose the Petroff Defense, counterattacking e4 instead of defending our e5 pawn.} 2...Nf6 3.Nxe5 {White grabs the e5 pawn with their knight. Let's enter the Gotham Petroff Gambit with 3...d5!} 3...d5 4.Nc3 {White develops the b1-knight, an option seen in just 3% of games. We push the d-pawn, gaining space and forcing the knight to move. A useful tempo.} 4...d4 5.Ne2 {The knight retreats to e2, adding pressure to the d4-pawn. Returning to its home square, b1, would be a bit sad.} 5...Nxe4 {We recapture on e4 with the knight, restoring material equality. The position is equal. We’ll develop the bishop to d6 and castle short, while the b8-knight develops to c6, helping to defend the d4 pawn. If White tries to kick our knight with d3, we’ll drop the bishop on b4, checking the king.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Alts to 3.Nxe5 / Study 1: White Plays 3.Nc3 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/840e407d-54cc-45e3-9f3e-bff84a26d66d/chapters/1bcea89b-e7e1-478b-b50c-4b2e044b6750/studies/96fa411f-a2c8-4736-955a-b896f83c6505/lines"]

1.e4 {We will now see early sidelines. In this study, we’ll take a look at what happens after 3.Nc3, a very popular move which is extremely important to be ready for. Let’s dive in! White begins with 1.e4. We mirror their move with 1...e5, taking equal space in the center.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3, instantly eyeing our e5-pawn. We go for the Petroff, challenging e4 directly instead of protecting e5.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {The knight comes to c3 31% of the time. White develops the knight and defends e4, avoiding the Petroff for now by not capturing on e5 and aiming instead for a different kind of game. We activate our bishop first and pressure the knight.} 3...Bb4 4.Nxe5 {White now decides it’s time to capture on e5. We don’t mind. We castle short, planning to bring the rook to the e-file and increase pressure.} 4...O-O 5.Nd3 {The knight steps back to d3, hitting our bishop. Not the most popular move. We'll capture the knight on c3. This actually allows us to win the pawn back in the center.} 5...Bxc3 6.dxc3 {White recaptures. We take back the pawn with the knight. Material is now equal.} 6...Nxe4 7.Be2 {White continues developing, bringing out the bishop to castle short, and possibly maneuvering it to f3 to increase pressure on our knight. We place our rook on the e-file, adding pressure and reinforcing the knight on e4.} 7...Re8 8.O-O {White castles short. We open the diagonal for our light-squared bishop.} 8...d6 9.Bf3 {As expected, White plays Bf3, adding pressure to our knight. We calmly develop and add a defender to the knight. We want it to stay on this powerful square.} 9...Bf5 10.Re1 {White doubles up the pressure, clearly trying to kick our knight from e4.} 10...Nc6 {But we calmly continue development. We’ve reached a very comfortable, equal position. A simple plan here is to bring out the queen to connect the rooks and double up along the e-file.} *

