1.e4 {We will now see early sidelines. In this study, we’ll take a look at what happens after 3.Nc3, a very popular move which is extremely important to be ready for. Let’s dive in! White begins with 1.e4. We mirror their move with 1...e5, taking equal space in the center.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3, instantly eyeing our e5-pawn. We go for the Petroff, challenging e4 directly instead of protecting e5.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {The knight comes to c3 31% of the time. White develops the knight and defends e4, avoiding the Petroff for now by not capturing on e5 and aiming instead for a different kind of game. We activate our bishop first and pressure the knight.} 3...Bb4 4.Nxe5 {White now decides it’s time to capture on e5. We don’t mind. We castle short, planning to bring the rook to the e-file and increase pressure.} 4...O-O 5.Bc4 {White plays another common option, developing the bishop and preparing to castle short. You should face this just under 20% of the time. We push our d-pawn, forcing the knight to retreat from e5.} 5...d6 6.Nf3 {The knight retreats. We capture the e4-pawn! It might look like we’ve just hung a knight, but it’s all part of the idea.} 6...Nxe4 7.Nxe4 {White accepts the “free” knight.} 7...d5 {Fork! We win the piece back immediately. From here, we’ll build pressure with ...Re8, ...Nc6, and ...Bg4. If White grabs the pawn with Bxd5 before returning the piece, it’s a mistake — we recapture with the queen, hit e4, and follow up with ...Re8, gaining the initiative.} *

