1.e4 {We will now see early sidelines. In this study, we’ll take a look at what happens after 3.Nc3, a very popular move which is extremely important to be ready for. Let’s dive in! White begins with 1.e4. We mirror their move with 1...e5, taking equal space in the center.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3, instantly eyeing our e5-pawn. We go for the Petroff, challenging e4 directly instead of protecting e5.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {The knight comes to c3 31% of the time. White develops the knight and defends e4, avoiding the Petroff for now by not capturing on e5 and aiming instead for a different kind of game. We activate our bishop first and pressure the knight.} 3...Bb4 4.Nxe5 {White now decides it’s time to capture on e5. We don’t mind. We castle short, planning to bring the rook to the e-file and increase pressure.} 4...O-O 5.d3 {White plays d3. It's the most popular choice, seen in 33% of games. But it’s not very ambitious, and White quickly falls behind in development.} 5...d5 {We already have pressure on the knight, so we strike in the center with ...d5, threatening ...d4 to be as disruptive as possible. Our plan also includes ...Re8 to increase pressure along the e-file, where the knight on e5 could end up pinned to the king, and developing our other knight via d7.} *

