1.e4 {We will now see early sidelines. In this study, we’ll take a look at what happens after 3.Nc3, a very popular move which is extremely important to be ready for. Let’s dive in! White begins with 1.e4. We mirror their move with 1...e5, taking equal space in the center.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3, instantly eyeing our e5-pawn. We go for the Petroff, challenging e4 directly instead of protecting e5.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {The knight comes to c3 31% of the time. White develops the knight and defends e4, avoiding the Petroff for now by not capturing on e5 and aiming instead for a different kind of game. We activate our bishop first and pressure the knight.} 3...Bb4 4.Bc4 {White develops the light-squared bishop with the intention to quickly castle short. It's the second most popular option, played in 25% of games. We support our e5-pawn, which was under attack.} 4...d6 5.d3 {White also reinforces their center.} 5...h6 {We stop any ideas of a bishop or knight jump to g5. The position is balanced, and we’re heading toward a normal middlegame. We’ll look to castle short and quickly trade on c3 to damage White’s pawn structure. We’ll also finish our development with ...Nc6 and ...Be6.} *

