1.e4 {We will now see early sidelines. In this study, we’ll take a look at what happens after 3.Nc3, a very popular move which is extremely important to be ready for. Let’s dive in! White begins with 1.e4. We mirror their move with 1...e5, taking equal space in the center.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops the knight to f3, instantly eyeing our e5-pawn. We go for the Petroff, challenging e4 directly instead of protecting e5.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {The knight comes to c3 31% of the time. White develops the knight and defends e4, avoiding the Petroff for now by not capturing on e5 and aiming instead for a different kind of game. We activate our bishop first and pressure the knight.} 3...Bb4 4.d3 {This is the most common response, played in 31% of games. While solid, it’s also a bit passive and doesn’t challenge our setup. We strike in the center immediately, and we're threatening to win the c3-knight with ...d4 and gain space, so White is forced to respond.} 4...d5 5.exd5 {White captures, which is the best response. We recapture with the knight, increasing the pressure on c3.} 5...Nxd5 6.Bd2 {White develops the dark-squared bishop while defending the c3-knight.} 6...Nc6 {We continue by developing the knight and reinforcing the e5-pawn. Our plan is to castle short, and if White allows it, we’ll trade on c3 to damage their pawn structure. The position is equal — about -0.1 according to the engine.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Alts to 3.Nxe5 / Study 2: White Plays 3.Bc4 or others / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/840e407d-54cc-45e3-9f3e-bff84a26d66d/chapters/1bcea89b-e7e1-478b-b50c-4b2e044b6750/studies/9614a505-5a71-46d2-ac2f-c51152aa3017/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of Chapter 5 in the Petroff Gotham Gambit. In this final study of the chapter, we’ll explore the remaining alternatives White has on move 3, which collectively appear in 34% of games. The moves we’ll cover are: 3.Bc4, 3.d4, and 3.d3. We also occupy the center with 1...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops the knight and targets our e5 pawn. And with this move, we enter the Petroff: directly challenging e4 instead of defending e5.} 2...Nf6 3.Bc4 {White brings out the light-squared bishop early, aiming for quick kingside castling. It’s actually a mistake — but still played in about 20% of games at amateur level! We'll capture the unprotected e4-pawn first.} 3...Nxe4 4.O-O {Instead of capturing our pawn, White sticks to their idea of castling short. That gives us a chance to strike in the center, gaining space, supporting our knight, and hitting the bishop.} 4...d5 5.Bb3 {The bishop retreats to safety. We develop our knight and successfully defend our e5-pawn, all while keeping our extra pawn. Black is already better.} 5...Nc6 6.Re1 {White brings the rook to the e-file, pressuring our knight and lining up with our king, but this move turns out to be a mistake. We develop and immediately target the now undefended f2-pawn.} 6...Bc5 7.d4 {White gives up the d-pawn to open the diagonal for the dark-squared bishop, aiming to use it to defend f2.} 7...Nxd4 {We simply capture it, going two pawns up and creating threats of discovered attacks on f2. We can castle short whenever we choose. If White plays 8.Be3, we respond with 8...Nxb3 and then trade dark-squared bishops, simplifying and gaining time to reinforce the center with ...f6.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Alts to 3.Nxe5 / Study 2: White Plays 3.Bc4 or others / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/840e407d-54cc-45e3-9f3e-bff84a26d66d/chapters/1bcea89b-e7e1-478b-b50c-4b2e044b6750/studies/9614a505-5a71-46d2-ac2f-c51152aa3017/lines"]

1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of Chapter 5 in the Petroff Gotham Gambit. In this final study of the chapter, we’ll explore the remaining alternatives White has on move 3, which collectively appear in 34% of games. The moves we’ll cover are: 3.Bc4, 3.d4, and 3.d3. We also occupy the center with 1...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops the knight and targets our e5 pawn. And with this move, we enter the Petroff: directly challenging e4 instead of defending e5.} 2...Nf6 3.d4 {This is a common response to the Petroff, played in 7% of games. But we have a line prepared, saving us study time. We take the central pawn and give White three main options in return.} 3...exd4 4.Qxd4 {White recaptures with the queen — the weakest of the three main options. After this, Black has fully equalized and holds a small initiative. We develop with tempo, attacking the queen.} 4...Nc6 5.Qe3 {The queen steps back. We develop the bishop with check. At first glance, it looks like White will block with c3, but we have a plan to reroute the bishop to b6, targeting the queen. If White blocks with a piece, we’ll simply castle short.} 5...Bb4+ 6.c3 {White blocks with the c-pawn.} 6...Ba5 {We retreat the bishop one step closer to completing the maneuver to b6, where it will target the queen. Our next steps include castling short and striking the center with ...d5.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Alts to 3.Nxe5 / Study 2: White Plays 3.Bc4 or others / Variation 4"]
