1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of Chapter 5 in the Petroff Gotham Gambit. In this final study of the chapter, we’ll explore the remaining alternatives White has on move 3, which collectively appear in 34% of games. The moves we’ll cover are: 3.Bc4, 3.d4, and 3.d3. We also occupy the center with 1...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops the knight and targets our e5 pawn. And with this move, we enter the Petroff: directly challenging e4 instead of defending e5.} 2...Nf6 3.d4 {This is a common response to the Petroff, played in 7% of games. But we have a line prepared, saving us study time. We take the central pawn and give White three main options in return.} 3...exd4 4.e5 {This is White’s best option, gaining space in the center and hitting our knight. We jump forward with the knight, planning to support it with ...d5.} 4...Ne4 5.Qxd4 {White captures on d4, centralizing the queen and attacking our knight. As planned, we support our knight with the d-pawn. If White isn’t careful, ideas like ...Bc5 — targeting the queen and f2 — could be very dangerous.} 5...d5 6.exd6 {White captures en passant. We recapture with the knight.} 6...Nxd6 7.Nc3 {White continues developing. We do the same, gaining tempo by hitting the queen.} 7...Nc6 8.Qf4 {The queen steps aside while staying active along the fourth rank.} 8...g6 {Our plan is to fianchetto the dark-squared bishop and castle short. We’ll likely see opposite-side castling, leading to a sharp game. The light-squared bishop will likely go to f5.} *

