1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of Chapter 5 in the Petroff Gotham Gambit. In this final study of the chapter, we’ll explore the remaining alternatives White has on move 3, which collectively appear in 34% of games. The moves we’ll cover are: 3.Bc4, 3.d4, and 3.d3. We also occupy the center with 1...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops the knight and targets our e5 pawn. And with this move, we enter the Petroff: directly challenging e4 instead of defending e5.} 2...Nf6 3.d4 {This is a common response to the Petroff, played in 7% of games. But we have a line prepared, saving us study time. We take the central pawn and give White three main options in return.} 3...exd4 4.Nxd4 {White recaptures with the knight, but this already allows us to equalize. We grab the e4-pawn with our knight.} 4...Nxe4 5.Qe2 {White pins our knight with the queen. It might look like we're losing a piece, but 5...Qe7 solves everything! The queen breaks the pin and defends the knight.} 5...Qe7 6.Nf5 {White jumps forward to attack our queen. We calmly advance the queen, attacking the knight and keeping our e4-knight protected.} 6...Qe6 7.Nd4 {White brings the knight back to d4, again with tempo. We avoid repetition and attack their knight, though ours is once again pinned.} 7...Qd5 8.c3 {If White plays more passively with 8.c3, we simply unpin the knight and continue development. Now we’re ready to castle short. We’re close to reaching a middlegame up a pawn and with a safe king.} 8...Be7 9.f3 {White kicks our knight back. We retreat to d6, preventing any Nb5 jumps.} 9...Nd6 10.Bf4 {White develops their bishop, attacking our knight.} 10...O-O {We simply castle short. Our plan is to continue development with ...Nc6, ...b6, and ...Bb7. Note that White cannot capture our bishop. After ...Re8, the queen would be pinned to the king and lost.} *

