1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of Chapter 5 in the Petroff Gotham Gambit. In this final study of the chapter, we’ll explore the remaining alternatives White has on move 3, which collectively appear in 34% of games. The moves we’ll cover are: 3.Bc4, 3.d4, and 3.d3. We also occupy the center with 1...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops the knight and targets our e5 pawn. And with this move, we enter the Petroff: directly challenging e4 instead of defending e5.} 2...Nf6 3.d4 {This is a common response to the Petroff, played in 7% of games. But we have a line prepared, saving us study time. We take the central pawn and give White three main options in return.} 3...exd4 4.Nxd4 {White recaptures with the knight, but this already allows us to equalize. We grab the e4-pawn with our knight.} 4...Nxe4 5.Qe2 {White pins our knight with the queen. It might look like we're losing a piece, but 5...Qe7 solves everything! The queen breaks the pin and defends the knight.} 5...Qe7 6.Nf5 {White jumps forward to attack our queen. We calmly advance the queen, attacking the knight and keeping our e4-knight protected.} 6...Qe6 7.Nd4 {White brings the knight back to d4, again with tempo. We avoid repetition and attack their knight, though ours is once again pinned.} 7...Qd5 8.Nb5 {White jumps forward with the knight, escaping danger and creating two threats: a fork on c7 and winning our pinned knight. It may look threatening, but we’ve got one move that keeps material equality and holds the position together.} 8...Kd8 {We unpin the knight and protect the c7-pawn. Yes, we’ve lost castling rights, but we’re up a clean pawn! One strong idea here is to play ...Bc5. If White tries 9.Bf4, we defend with 9...Bb4+ followed by ...Ba5, repositioning and holding everything together.} *

