1.e4 {Welcome to the second study of Chapter 5 in the Petroff Gotham Gambit. In this final study of the chapter, we’ll explore the remaining alternatives White has on move 3, which collectively appear in 34% of games. The moves we’ll cover are: 3.Bc4, 3.d4, and 3.d3. We also occupy the center with 1...e5.} 1...e5 2.Nf3 {White develops the knight and targets our e5 pawn. And with this move, we enter the Petroff: directly challenging e4 instead of defending e5.} 2...Nf6 3.d3 {This move appears in 7% of games. It supports the e4 pawn and opens the diagonal for the dark-squared bishop, but leaves the light-squared bishop stuck inside the pawn chain.} 3...Nc6 {We develop our knight and defend e5, keeping our d-pawn flexible for a later ...d5 push. This also allows us to bring out the dark-squared bishop before locking it in and look to castle short. We’ve already equalized here.} *